PULPissimo Verilator test environment: change log
===============================================

Purpose: context for a future session (human or Claude) picking this work up.
For user-facing usage see README.md in this folder.

2026-10-26: initial environment
-------------------------------

GOAL
  The user's simulator is Verilator. Upstream PULPissimo supports only
  Questa and Xcelium; the README says Verilator support is "planned, but not
  finished". The user wanted a `tests/` folder where tests can be dropped in
  and run. Hard constraint from the user: DO NOT modify original/tracked
  code. Everything lives in tests/ (plus gitignored dependency checkouts).

USER'S MACHINE
  - Verilator 5.053 (devel) at /usr/local/bin/verilator
  - riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc 16.1 (stock, in ~/riscv32/bin)
      * no PULP extensions (xcorev / xpulpv2 / xgap9 all rejected)
      * defaults to C23, and pulp-runtime fails under C23 (K&R `int (*)()`
        prototypes)
      * multilibs: hard-float only as rv32imafc/ilp32f
  - No Questa or Xcelium

ACTIONS OUTSIDE tests/ (all gitignored, no tracked file touched)
  - Ran `make checkout` in the repo root. It installed bender into
    utils/bin/bender (got 0.31.0, although utils.mk asks for 0.28.0) and
    checked out all IPs into .bender/.

FILES CREATED
  tests/Makefile                    Top-level flow: hw / sw / run / all-tests / clean
  tests/README.md                   User documentation
  tests/.gitignore                  ignores build/
  tests/changes.log                 this file
  tests/env/tb_verilator.sv         Testbench top (module tb_verilator)
  tests/env/clock_gen_verilator.sv  Drop-in `module clock_gen` replacement
  tests/env/pad_functional_verilator.sv  pad_functional_pu/pd replacements
  tests/env/elf2mem.py              ELF -> "entry\naddr data\n..." image
  tests/env/sw.mk                   Builds one test via sw/pulp-runtime
  tests/hello/hello.c               printf + return 0
  tests/l2_preload/l2_preload.c     96 KiB const table spanning pri0, pri1 and
                                    interleaved L2; verifies backdoor mapping

HOW IT WORKS
  Build (Makefile, target `hw`, output build/sim-<core>/Vtb_verilator):
    1. `bender -d <root> script verilator --no-default-target
        -t rtl -t rtl_sim -t verilator -e adv_dbg_if -e tbtools`
       then drop deprecated/pad_functional.sv, and append:
       env/pad_functional_verilator.sv, hw/vendored_ips/gpio/src/gpio_input_stage.sv,
       adv_dbg_if rtl files (listed by hand) + incdir, build/.../padframe_adapter.sv
       (patched copy), env/clock_gen_verilator.sv, env/tb_verilator.sv
    2. verilator --binary --timing --trace-fst, top tb_verilator,
       -GCORE_TYPE / -GUSE_FPU from CORE=cv32e40p|ibex, many -Wno-* (see Makefile)
    files.f depends on the Makefile, so editing the Makefile rebuilds the model.

  Software (target `sw`, output build/sw-<core>/<test>/):
    make -C tests/<test> -f env/sw.mk TEST=<test> TARGET_BUILD_DIR=...
    sw.mk exports the env vars normally set by
    `source sw/pulp-runtime/configs/pulpissimo_cv32.sh`, compiles every
    *.c/*.S in the test folder, and includes an optional per-test test.mk.
    Flags for stock GCC:
      cv32e40p: CFLAGS -march=rv32imfc_zicsr_zifencei -mabi=ilp32f -std=gnu11
                LDFLAGS -march=rv32imafc_zicsr_zifencei -mabi=ilp32f
                (link with "a" only so GCC picks the ilp32f libgcc multilib;
                 the code itself has no atomics, which the core lacks)
      ibex:     rv32imc_zicsr_zifencei / ilp32
    Then elf2mem.py writes <test>.mem and objdump writes <test>.dis.

  Simulation (target `run`): Vtb_verilator +MEM=... +TIMEOUT_US=... [+WAVES=f.fst]
    tb_verilator.sv:
      - ~32.768 kHz ref clock; pads connected through wires (Verilator rejects
        constants on inout ports)
      - bootsel=3, JTAG held in reset
      - while in reset, backdoor-writes L2 SRAM arrays:
          i_dut.i_soc_domain.i_pulp_soc.l2_ram_i.bank_sram_pri0_i.sram  (0x1C000000, 8K words)
          ...bank_sram_pri1_i.sram                                      (0x1C008000, 8K words)
          ...CUTS[0..3].bank_i.sram  interleaved from 0x1C010000:
                                     bank = addr[3:2], row = off[17:4]
        (tc_sram with SimInit="none" does not clear contents on reset)
      - `force i_dut.i_soc_domain.i_pulp_soc.s_fc_bootaddr = entry` to skip the
        bootrom (the FC_BOOTADDR register resets to the bootrom at 0x1A000080)
      - end of test: watches
          ...soc_peripherals_i.s_soc_ctrl_reg_reg2hw.corestatus.{eoc,exit_code}.q
        (pulp-runtime exit() writes CORESTATUS at 0x1A1040A0)
        exit 0 -> $finish (process rc 0), otherwise $fatal (rc != 0)
        timeout -> $fatal
      - printf output: the SIM_STDOUT virtual peripheral (tb_fs_handler in
        pulp_soc), printed as "[STDOUT-CL31_PE0, <time>] ..."

    clock_gen_verilator.sv:
      soc_clk and per_clk come from `always #10ns` (50 MHz). It keeps apb_to_fll
      and stubs each FLL's 4 registers (status, conf1, conf2, integrator) with
      runtime-compatible reset values. ack is req delayed by one cycle, and
      rdata is latched on the rising edge of req, because apb_to_fll zeroes
      addr once ack is seen. Lock is always 1.

UPSTREAM PROBLEMS WORKED AROUND (originals untouched)
  1. The FLL model (generic_FLL) is VHDL, so it can't be used in Verilator.
     Bender.yml already excludes clock_gen_generic.sv and padframe_adapter.sv
     for the `verilator` target; we supply both ourselves.
  2. `bender script verilator` implicitly adds the `synthesis` target, which
     drops tb_fs_handler (printf) and tech_cells deprecated cells. Fix:
     --no-default-target.
  3. adv_dbg_if's Bender.yml has the typo "rtl/adbg_axi_biu.sv," (trailing
     comma). bender 0.31 raises E31 (file doesn't exist). The package is still
     needed (pulp_soc lint_jtag_wrap uses adbg_lintonly_top), so it is
     excluded and its files are listed in the Makefile.
  4. tech_cells_generic deprecated/pad_functional.sv uses rpmos/bufif0, which
     Verilator doesn't support. Replaced by env/pad_functional_verilator.sv
     (always pulled; PEN ignored).
  5. hw/padframe/padframe_adapter.sv:140 has a $error with 2x %0d but only 1
     argument. Verilator raises a hard error. The Makefile sed-patches a copy
     into build/sim-<core>/padframe_adapter.sv.
  6. tbtools dpi_models.sv fails to elaborate (QSPI interface members), and
     it is Questa-only VIP, so the package is excluded.
  7. With Ibex, pulp_soc fc_subsystem.sv passes ints to enum parameters
     (ENUMVALUE error), so -Wno-ENUMVALUE is used.
  8. Verilator quirk: a comment whose text starts with "Verilator" at the top of a
     .sv file is parsed as a pragma (BADVLTPRAGMA). The headers are worded to
     avoid it.

VERIFIED
  - cv32e40p: hello PASS, l2_preload PASS (`make all-tests` rc=0)
  - ibex:     hello PASS, l2_preload PASS
  - return 3 from main -> FAILED, rc != 0; infinite loop -> TIMEOUT, rc != 0
  - WAVES=1 produces an FST
  - Model build ~90 s on 8 threads; sim ~350-500 us simulated per wall second
    (~20k cycles/s); hello takes ~2 s end-to-end.

KNOWN LIMITATIONS / IDEAS FOR LATER
  - No JTAG/debug-module boot path (backdoor only).
  - Fixed clocks; FLL programming has no effect on real frequency.
  - No external device models (flash, HyperRAM, camera, I2S); UART RX pad
    only pulled up. Peripheral tests need models added to tb_verilator.sv.
  - printf goes through virtual stdout, not the UART.
  - build/lint.log is a stale artifact from the first bring-up lint runs and
    can be ignored or deleted (build/ is generated; `make clean` removes it).


2026-10-26: physical hardware, cycle accuracy, area/power (notes, nothing built yet)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STATUS
  Research only; no files changed. The user has NO JTAG adapter right now.
  The user's board is undecided/unconfirmed: Nexys Video or Nexys 4 DDR.
  Software-only for now. Open offers:
    (a) a perf.h cycle-counting helper plus an example test
    (b) a Vivado area/power target in tests/
    (c) UART program loading for the board (option C below)

BOARD SUPPORT (upstream target/fpga/)
  Nexys Video   `make nexys_video` in target/fpga; xc7a200tsbg484-1.
                The SoC JTAG is routed to channel 0 of the ON-BOARD FTDI
                (PROG micro-USB J12), so NO external adapter is needed.
                Config: target/fpga/pulpissimo-nexys_video/openocd-nexys_video.cfg
  Nexys 4 DDR   `make nexys rev=nexys4DDR`; xc7a100tcsg324-1 (the L2 is
                4x64 KiB + 2x32 KiB = 320 KiB, which fits in BRAM).
                The SoC JTAG is on PMOD JA pins 1-6
                (TMS, TDI, TDO, TCK, GND, VCC), so an EXTERNAL adapter is
                required.
  Both          FC clock 10 MHz, peripheral clock 5 MHz (fpga-settings.mk),
                UART on the on-board USB-UART, CPU reset button.
                bootsel is tied to 0 in run.tcl. The bootrom therefore starts
                the zforth shell on UART (zforth takes priority over UART-srec
                boot in sw/bootcode/boot_code.c).
  Risks         Upstream FPGA scripts target an older Vivado than the
                installed 2026.1 (/mnt/depo/Xilinx/2026.1); the clk_wiz IPs
                may need regenerating. OpenOCD is NOT installed (it needs the
                riscv target and ftdi support). riscv32-unknown-elf-gdb is
                installed.

WAYS TO GET A PROGRAM ONTO THE BOARD
  A. JTAG + OpenOCD + GDB (upstream flow, see elf_run.gdb). GDB loads the
     ELF into L2, sets the PC and continues. The exit status is read from
     CORESTATUS 0x1A1040A0 over JTAG; printf output comes from the UART.
     Best for tests: seconds per test, no rebuild, and a real debugger.
     Planned shape:
       make run TEST=x PLATFORM=fpga BOARD=nexys_video
     with the same PASS/FAIL exit code semantics as the simulator.
  B. Program in the bitstream: L2 BRAM init (Vivado updatemem, needs an
     MMI) plus a boot address change. One bitstream per program. Only
     useful for a standalone demo.
  C. UART loading without JTAG: rebuild the FPGA bootrom without
     ENABLE_ZFORTH_BOOT so the UART-srec boot path is used. Build that
     bootrom in tests/, NOT via `make bootrom`, which overwrites
     hw/fpga_autogen_rom.sv. One bitstream rebuild, then any test can be
     sent over the USB-UART. No debugger, and the exit code must be
     reported over UART. This is the no-adapter option for the
     Nexys 4 DDR.
  D. QSPI flash boot (bootsel=2). Standalone deployment only.
  Shared sim/hw tests would need PLATFORM=sim|fpga in sw.mk. For the FPGA,
  pulp-runtime is built with platform=fpga, which turns on CONFIG_IO_UART=1,
  so printf goes to the real UART.

CYCLE ACCURACY: SIMULATION VS. HARDWARE
  The core, interconnect, L2 and peripherals are the same RTL, so core-cycle
  counts for code that only touches the core and L2 are identical in
  simulation and on the FPGA. Differences:
  - Clocks: the sim runs SoC and peripheral clocks at 50 MHz; the FPGA runs
    10 MHz (FC) and 5 MHz (peripherals). Compare cycles, not time.
    Peripheral/uDMA latency in core cycles differs because the
    core:peripheral clock ratio is different.
  - printf: the sim uses the virtual stdout (a few cycles); the FPGA uses
    UART at 115200 baud, about 870 core cycles per character at 10 MHz.
    Never time code that prints.
  - Clock generation: the sim stub reports the FLL locked instantly, while
    the FPGA uses Xilinx clocking. Only startup before main() is affected.
  - Waits based on wall time (timer, ms) differ in cycle count.
  Method: read mcycle (cycle CSR) around the region of interest and print
  the result afterwards.

AREA AND POWER
  Verilator gives function only, no area or power. Options:
  A. Vivado (recommended; available locally). report_utilization after
     synthesis/implementation gives LUT/FF/BRAM/DSP per hierarchy.
     report_power with no activity data is a vectorless guess. For
     per-test power, dump waves from the Verilator run over the region of
     interest, convert to SAIF (or use VCD if the Vivado version accepts
     it), read it into Vivado with the TB hierarchy mapped onto the FPGA
     top, and run report_power. The TB path is tb_verilator.i_dut; the FPGA
     top is xilinx_pulpissimo.i_pulpissimo. The results are FPGA numbers,
     good for relative comparisons between design changes, not for
     predicting an ASIC.
  B. ASIC-style flow: Yosys (needs sv2v or yosys-slang for this SV) plus
     OpenROAD/OpenSTA with a public PDK (e.g. SKY130), for area in um^2
     and activity-annotated power. Much more effort: the SRAMs need memory
     macros or they synthesize to huge flip-flop arrays, and the run times
     are long.

JTAG ADAPTER: DIGILENT JTAG-HS2
  Compatible. The Nexys 4 target was designed around it: the PMOD JA
  pinout matches the HS2 6-pin header, and upstream ships
  target/fpga/pulpissimo-nexys/openocd-nexys-hs2.cfg (FTDI 0403:6014).
  That cfg uses the old OpenOCD syntax (`interface ftdi`, `adapter_khz`);
  current OpenOCD needs `adapter driver ftdi`, `adapter speed`, etc.
  Adapt it in a copy under tests/. Not needed for the Nexys Video, which
  has on-board JTAG.
